# netsurf: streaming fetch() bodies -- ReadableStream, Response.body, fetch at first bytes
#
# North-star B1, part 2. Apps read an event stream as
# fetch(url).then(r => r.body.getReader()) (the Anthropic SDK, every
# "stream the tokens" UI). The bridge's fetch() resolved only at DONE with
# a text-only Response: no body, no arrayBuffer, and no ReadableStream in
# the engine at all.
#
# - macos9_js_fetch.c: __xhrNativeTakeBytes(id, discard), the new body
#   bytes as an ArrayBuffer (exact bytes: no UTF-8 decode, binary safe),
#   and __redirects on the slot's JS object (Response.redirected; comparing
#   URLs misfires on normalisation).
# - vita_polyfill.js, each only where nothing better exists:
#   ReadableStream (default reader, read/cancel/releaseLock/closed, pull
#   sources, async iteration with cancel on break, tee, pipeTo,
#   pipeThrough, ReadableStream.from), a minimal WritableStream and
#   TransformStream, TextDecoderStream / TextEncoderStream.
# - Response: body is a ReadableStream of Uint8Array (lazily made for
#   string / bytes / Blob bodies); text, json, bytes, arrayBuffer, blob,
#   formData (url-encoded), clone (tees a streamed body), bodyUsed,
#   Response.error / json / redirect; Content-Type from the body kind.
# - fetch(): the request starts at call time (after reading a Blob or
#   stream body), resolves at the first body bytes (or at DONE for an
#   empty body), feeds response.body from the progress ticks with
#   discarding takes, closes it at DONE, errors it with TypeError on a
#   network failure. reader.cancel() and AbortSignal abort the native
#   fetch; an abort after resolution errors the body with the signal's
#   reason. Headers and statusText come from the slot's raw header block.
#   A body on GET/HEAD rejects, as the spec says.
#
# Known limits: request bodies go to the fetcher as a string (a binary
# upload is decoded as UTF-8); no backpressure (the network fills the
# queue as fast as it arrives); fetch resolves at the first body byte, not
# at the header block.
#
# testpages/pages/JS-fetch-stream.html (NET-js-fetch-stream{,-https}).
